The behavior you're seeing is normal: the model *always* generates some text response, and whether it calls a tool is entirely its decision. You can't force Ollama/Qwen to return a fixed string — but you **can detect on the Spring AI side that no tool was called and substitute your own message**. There are a few ways to do it:

## Option 1: User-controlled tool execution (recommended)

Disable automatic tool execution, check `hasToolCalls()` yourself, and if the model didn't call a tool, discard its answer and return your custom message:

```java
ToolCallingManager toolCallingManager = ToolCallingManager.builder().build();

ChatOptions chatOptions = ToolCallingChatOptions.builder()
    .toolCallbacks(ToolCallbacks.from(new MyTools()))
    .internalToolExecutionEnabled(false)   // Spring AI 1.0.x
    .build();

Prompt prompt = new Prompt(userQuestion, chatOptions);
ChatResponse chatResponse = chatModel.call(prompt);

if (!chatResponse.hasToolCalls()) {
    // Model decided not to call any tool -> return YOUR message
    return "Sorry, I can only help with X. Please rephrase your request.";
}

// Otherwise run the normal tool loop
while (chatResponse.hasToolCalls()) {
    ToolExecutionResult result = toolCallingManager.executeToolCalls(prompt, chatResponse);
    prompt = new Prompt(result.conversationHistory(), chatOptions);
    chatResponse = chatModel.call(prompt);
}
return chatResponse.getResult().getOutput().getText();
```

> ⚠️ Version note: `internalToolExecutionEnabled(false)` exists in Spring AI 1.x. In newer versions (2.x+) that flag was removed — instead you just call `ChatModel` directly (tools aren't auto-executed there), or with `ChatClient` disable the advisor via `.advisors(AdvisorParams.toolCallingAdvisorAutoRegister(false))`.

## Option 2: An Advisor that intercepts the response

If you want this behavior reusable across your app, wrap the logic in a custom `CallAdvisor`:

```java
public class FallbackMessageAdvisor implements CallAdvisor {

    @Override
    public ChatClientResponse adviseCall(ChatClientRequest request, CallAdvisorChain chain) {
        ChatClientResponse response = chain.nextCall(request);
        ChatResponse chatResponse = response.chatResponse();

        boolean toolWasUsed = chatResponse.getResults().stream()
            .anyMatch(g -> g.getOutput().hasToolCalls());

        if (!toolWasUsed) {
            // replace the model's text with your fixed message
            AssistantMessage custom = new AssistantMessage("Sorry, I can only answer questions about X.");
            ChatResponse replaced = ChatResponse.builder()
                .generations(List.of(new Generation(custom)))
                .build();
            return response.mutate().chatResponse(replaced).build();
        }
        return response;
    }

    @Override public String getName() { return "fallbackAdvisor"; }
    @Override public int getOrder() { return Ordered.LOWEST_PRECEDENCE; }
}
```

Note: to detect "no tool called" reliably with an advisor, it must run *inside/before* the tool-execution loop finishes — with auto tool execution the final response never contains tool calls (they're consumed internally). So Option 1 gives you cleaner control; the advisor approach works best when combined with disabled internal execution.

## Option 3: Prompt-level control (softer, less reliable)

Add a system prompt like:

```
If the user's request does not match any available tool,
respond with exactly: "Sorry, I can only help with X."
Do not answer anything else.
```

This often works, but with smaller Qwen models on Ollama it's not guaranteed — the model may still improvise. That's why combining it with Option 1 (a hard programmatic check) is the robust solution.

## Summary

The key insight: **you can't make the model return a fixed message, but you can detect the absence of a tool call (`chatResponse.hasToolCalls() == false`) and replace its output with your own string before returning it to the user.** Option 1 is the standard Spring AI way to do this.
